Position Summary

The Project Engineer assists the PM of the MSC Cruise Terminal Project by organizing and controlling project

elements in the execution of certain activities as indicated below.

REPORTING

Hierarchical and Functional to: PM

Essential Duties And Responsibilities

 Participating in the elaboration of project planning, project schedule, 2 weeks’ advance schedule, project

budget and their following revisions, as requested by the PM;

 Assisting the PM in the project contractual administration and in the analysis and interpretation of

contract documents;

 Supporting the PM in the preparation and implementation of the Health & Safety and Environment

Program (HSE) in compliance with all applicable regulations (OSHA, etc.) as well as all other federal, state

and local statutes, regulations, standards and in accordance with the requirements of the Main Contract;

 Enforcing the observance of the QHSE rules at the Job Site;

 Assisting the PM in the preparation of contractual correspondence to Client and Client’s Representative

(Engineer/CEI);

 Generating the supporting documentation for change orders, supplemental agreements, claims, etc. and

relevant cost breakdowns as necessary for their approval;

 Dealing with Client, Client’s Representatives on site and subcontractors;

 Systematically reviewing the documentation regarding nonconformities, claims, and observations by the

Client and Client rep, in order to detect situations liable to compromise quality;

 Verifying the implementation of corrective actions as required;

 Monitor and supervise the dredging activities and related quantities, means and method.

 Dredge production monitoring, analysis & reporting

 Daily reporting & maintenance of any dredge instrumentation & automation

 Collaborating with the PM to transfer the QA/QC requirements to the Suppliers/Subcontractors.

 Supervising and coordinating the Project QA/QC activities performed by FIF and/or those performed by

Subcontractors:

 Identifying in a timely manner the over-runs/under-runs of pay items quantity;

 Based on the production data provided by General Superintendent, updating the baseline schedule and

the internal schedule;

 Reviewing all invoices related to works, materials and services issued by vendors and suppliers and

verifying their proper cost coding and phasing;

 Participating to the site meetings with the Client, its Representatives and subs;

 Preparing close-out documents;

 Receiving, logging, checking, submitting and maintaining shop drawings;

 Organizing subcontractor/vendor coordination meetings;

 Evaluating the compliance of subcontractor/vendor progress with the project schedule;

 Notifying for changed site conditions, requesting change orders, notifying claim, requesting time

extension, requesting certificate of substantial completion, requesting final acceptance certificate, etc.;

 Generating monthly project status reports;

 Coordinating with Engineer, City Inspectors and other Authorities to obtain required work permits.

Bilingual Spanish/English Program Director - San Gabriel - Des Plaines 60016


 Bilingual English/Spanish language competency required
 Master's degree in social work, or equivalent in education, psychology, sociology
 Bachelor's degree from an accredited college or university + five years' experience in child welfare administration.
 Must have valid Illinois Driver's License



The Program Director for theLong-Term Group Home (LTGH) is responsible for the overall management of programmatic, administrative, financial, and operational systems related to the provision of care and services. This position provides regular and timely reports to the Office of Refugee Resettlement (ORR) regarding operations, services and finances, establishes a respectful and supportive workplace environment, and communicates any issues or concerns to ORR. The Program Director is a grant position related solely to the Unaccompanied Children's Shelter as funded by the Office of Refugee Resettlement and any successor funding.


ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS:


 Conducts weekly meetings with program staff and administrators to facilitate resources and services for youth and staff;
 Develops and maintains a team approach to the day-to-day functioning of the program. Demonstrates skills in team development, collaboration, and open communication;
 Assures implementation of all Maryville Policy and Procedures and ensures the adherence to agency hiring and disciplinary process policies and procedures;
 Ensures in collaboration with HR that all staff have appropriate credentials, training, and expertise to do the work necessary. Collaborates with clinical directors, staff education, and external experts to facilitate training staff in areas of deficiencies;
 Coordinates with Administrative Services maintaining the physical plant, landscaping and agency vans/vehicles. Ensures that the environment of care is conducive to general health, well-being, and growth;
 Manages all program, budgetary and administrative operations for site(s) under his/her direction;
 Other duties as assigned.


MINIMUM REQUIREMENTS:

Education/Experience:


 Master's degree in social work, or an equivalent degree in education, psychology, sociology or other relevant behavioral science degree from an accredited college or university and three years' work experience with children, at least two of which were in institutional or other group care programs and at least two additional years of administrative experience preferred; or
 Bachelor's degree from an accredited college or university plus five years' experience in child welfare administration, child protective services, program management, or as a director of a licensed childcare program.




Skills /Knowledge:


 Bilingual English/Spanish language competency required;
 Successful completion of initial and ongoing training requirements;
 Ability to communicate effectively, verbally and in writing, with Maryville youth, supervisors, coworkers, subordinates, support agencies, and a variety of internal as well as external consumers;
 Ability to analytically problem-solve a variety of situations;
 Ability to cope with crisis situations in a calm, effective manner;
 Must possess a valid driver's license.


Physical Demands:


 Required to sit, stand, bend, stoop, and walk for extended periods; able to walk up and down stairs;
 Required to infrequently lift/move objects and/or youth weighing in excess of 40 pounds;
 Must have visual, hearing, and learning capabilities sufficient to perform the essential functions defined above.


Environment and Scheduling:


 Will perform work in, and in association with, Maryville program settings;
 Duties involve contact with youth who may be traumatized;
 Must be flexible in scheduling and willing to work evening and weekend shifts.
